## Hard rules
- READ-ONLY. Do not modify, create, move or delete any file except your one report file
  `doc/plan/c016-worklog/spec-review/R7-fw-relocation-observability-summary.md`. Never touch `core/**`, `bindings/**`,
  `framework/**`, any `doc/spec/**`, `framework/doc/**`, `doc/site/**`. Do not run builds, tests,
  benchmarks or git commands that change state (`git diff`/`git log`/`git grep` are fine). Do not commit.
- Evidence, not opinion: every finding cites spec `file:line` for *every* place the rule is stated and
  implementation `file:line` per language you examined. If you could not verify a language, say so.
- One finding = one cause. Do not propose mitigations (timeouts, retries, extra pollers, catch-alls).
  Every proposal is "N rules → M rules" with the draft sentence of the merged rule.
- Mark `행동 변경: 있음` whenever an application could observe a difference; the supervisor schedules
  those for 0.18.0 and applies only `없음` items now — be precise.
- Do not rewrite spec text at length; a proposal is one merged sentence plus where it lives.

## Scope (read all of it)
`framework/doc/framework/common/spec/server/05-location-relocation/*.ko.md` (location runtime, location store redis, relocation store redis, relocation flow, host relocation flow incl. §14 shutdown seal, failure/failover policy) and `06-observability/*.ko.md` (≈5,700 lines)

Implementation evidence: `framework/languages/{cpp,dotnet,java,node,kotlin}/**` location/relocation/shutdown runtimes; `framework/runtime/conformance/**` (relocation conformance fixtures)

## What to look for in this scope
- Relocation flow has many states, fences, deadlines and CAS steps (S0…, unit seal, temporary queue, cursor, fencing deadline, drain deadline). For each state/deadline: what caller-observable outcome does it produce that no other state produces? States with no distinct observable outcome are `complexity` candidates. Build the table.
- Shutdown §14 (admission seal, D-097/D-098 mesh follows the seal, D-098-4 no timed wait after publication): the seal is meant to be *one* judgment consulted by host, mesh, relocation. Verify in each language's code that there is one seal source (cite file:line) — multiple flags = `scattered-control`.
- Store contracts (redis location/relocation): are there client-side re-validations of values the store's CAS already guarantees? (`lower-layer-reverification`)
- Failure/failover policy vs error model (R4) vs routing §2.6: who owns "owner unavailable → Unavailable"? cross-reference R6.
- Observability: metrics/tracing/monitoring documents — any *behavioral* rule hidden in observability docs (must move to the owning spec), and `gate-drift`: `scripts/run-framework-relocation-conformance.mjs --list` fails at baseline (dotnet `SessionActorCoordinatorTests` lacks `Canonical_Seal_Retries_Target_Push_Until_Command_44_Commits`) — diagnose whether the fixture or the spec/tests drifted, report only.

